lessons.body:
MVC — Model, View, Controller — is the pattern Apple's frameworks assume: UIKit's `UIViewController` and SwiftUI's `View` both expect you to hold state and react to events in one place. The trouble isn't the split itself, it's what accumulates in the controller once network calls, validation, and navigation all need somewhere to live.

### The massive view controller

Nothing in MVC limits what a controller may do, so it becomes the default dumping ground: fetch logic, delegate callbacks, `UITableViewDataSource`, and presentation formatting all end up in the same file. The class grows until it's effectively untestable — exercising its logic means standing up a `UIViewController` and its full view hierarchy.

### MVVM: pulling logic out of the controller

MVVM adds a view model between the view and the model: a plain object holding presentation state and logic, with no reference to `UIView` or `UIViewController`. The view binds to it, and the view model becomes trivially testable, since it's just an object you can construct and assert against with no UI involved.

```swift
@Observable final class ProfileViewModel {
    private(set) var name = ""
    private let store: ProfileStoring

    init(store: ProfileStoring) { self.store = store }

    func load(id: String) async throws {
        name = try await store.fetch(id: id).name
    }
}
```

### MV: SwiftUI's shortcut

`@Observable` lets a `View` read model properties directly with automatic diffing, so a view model that only forwards `Model` properties one-for-one is often just ceremony. "MV" drops that view model where a screen's logic is thin, and keeps MVVM's separation where there's real logic worth isolating and testing on its own.

> KEY: The pattern is a lever for testability, not a religion — reach for MVVM when a screen has logic worth testing in isolation, and skip the ceremony when it doesn't.

A coordinator owns navigation decisions so a view doesn't have to know what comes after it. Without one, a view ends up holding a link to a specific destination view, which means it knows the whole flow it's part of — hard to reuse, harder to test, and impossible to change without touching every view along the path.

### What the coordinator holds

The coordinator decides *where* to go; the view only says *that* something happened — a tap, a completion, an error — usually through a closure or a delegate. That inversion is the entire pattern: the view exposes intent, the coordinator interprets it.

```swift
enum Route: Hashable {
    case profile(id: String)
    case settings
}

@Observable final class AppCoordinator {
    var path: [Route] = []
    func showProfile(id: String) { path.append(.profile(id: id)) }
}
```

### NavigationStack and path-based routing

SwiftUI's `NavigationStack(path:)` binds to exactly this kind of array: append a `Route` to push, mutate the array to pop to any point, or replace it wholesale to jump straight to a deep destination. `.navigationDestination(for:)` maps each `Route` case to the view that renders it, so the view layer never imports the coordinator's routing logic — it only reacts to `path` changing.

### Deep links

A deep link is navigation state expressed before the app is even running. If routing already flows through one array of `Route` values, handling a link is just: parse the URL into a `Route`, and set `path` to reach it — no different from a normal push, and testable without a UI at all, since the array can be asserted on directly.

> KEY: A view that knows the concrete destination it's pushing to isn't reusable — knowing only that it can *ask* to navigate is what a coordinator buys you.

